---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import euler1910 from '../assets/photos/horizon-euler1910.png';

/**
 * PS-5 — the horizon: ONE public-domain plate, captioned, standing on the section break between the club's
 * reasons and the schedule.
 *
 * Round-4 brief §7.2 (2026-11-15, D347) is what put one plate here. The three-leaf ruling below is kept as the
 * record of what was decided and why it stopped: the brief's own words — "the plates are the site's identity;
 * three of them in a row is an exhibition, and an exhibition is not a club" — and the measurement that priced
 * the exhibition: @measured 2026-11-15 by `node design/plates-price.mjs --no-build` on this build, the one plate
 * this commit ships costs **+320px at 390** (band 319.6px tall, leaf 358px filling the 390px column, page 4152px)
 * and **+473px at 1240** (band 472.8px, leaf 698x351 at its own scan, page 2905px); the two rungs between cost
 * +419px at 640 (band 419.5px, leaf 592px of the 592px column) and +284px at 320 (band 284.4px, leaf 288px).
 * Against the artifact this commit replaces, the same probe printed **three plates stacked at 390 added +715px of
 * page** - D329's band, kept in the header as of 5cd128f and quoted here as its record, not as this page; the two
 * figures are close by arithmetic (D329's three-leaf rungs vs one leaf at a wider cap), not by coincidence-checking.
 *
 * **D351 (2026-11-15) superseded that cap the same day D347 wrote it, and the operator's rulings letter of 2026-11-16
 * (R2, D356) undoes that undo: D351's cap deletion is undone and D347's cap is reinstated.** "Full-bleed" was a visual
 * outcome the archive cannot supply — the widest rung for this leaf is `/img/1024/` and that rung returns the leaf's OWN
 * scan, 698x351 (`/img/1600/`, `/img/2048/` and `/img/full/` answer 404, probed 2026-11-03) — so full-bleed at 1240 is
 * 1.71x interpolated pixels of the one asset whose whole value is hairlines. Capped, the plate paints at no more than
 * 1.0x its widest available source rung, and the plate plus its caption cost the page **+377px at 1240** - the band's own
 * box is 472.8px, of which 96px is declared `py-12` air, and the leaf paints 698x351, exactly 1.0x the archive's 698x351
 * scan, on a 2905px page @measured 2026-11-16 by `node design/plates-price.mjs --no-build`. Those figures are the
 * 2026-11-16 shape: the 2026-11-17 spacing ruling (S061, row F) takes this band's own air from 96px to 64px, so
 * every page figure in this paragraph is 32px shorter than a build of this tree measures. That is inside the 400px the
 * letter's replacement acceptance names, counting the plate rather than the air around it; the band's whole box, air
 * included, is 472.8px, and this header says which of the two it means wherever it quotes a figure. Against D351's
 * **band 721.2px holding a leaf 1192x599.4 on a 3153px page** - a 23% slice of the document for a plate about the
 * history of geometry, and +248px of page over what ships.
 *
 * What that refusal costs, measured on both shapes - @measured 2026-11-16 by `node design/plates-price.mjs --no-build`
 * (Linux native, Node 24.22.1). What ships is the capped band: at 1240 the band is 472.8px, the leaf 698x351, the page
 * 2905px; at 640, 419.5 / 592x297.7 on a 3932px page; at 390, 319.6 / 358x180 on a 4152px page; at 320, 284.4 /
 * 288x144.8 on a 4600px page. D351's full-bleed shape differs from it at exactly ONE width: at 1240 it prints 721.2 /
 * 1192x599.4 on a 3153px page, and below 1024 it prints the same boxes as the capped band, because the column is
 * narrower than the 698px scan and the cap never binds. So the 1.71x upscale that made full-bleed a refusal was the
 * one width band that shape reached, and it was reached only on the widest columns. Half of record 0023's caution
 * still applies and nothing here argues the other half: under the cap the leaf is never upscaled, so the hairlines stay
 * one-to-one with the archive's drawing at 1240 and shrink with it below, and `check:ground` - which runs in `verify`,
 * and is green on this build - is the instrument that decides whether they stay legible on the bytes a browser paints.
 *
 * Why three and not one used to be the ruling (this is the composition the site arrived at, 2026-11-03, round
 * 14 option C -> F): the archive serves a widest rung of `/img/1024/`, but that rung returns each leaf's *own*
 * scan width — the 1910 Euler leaf is 698x350 and the Byrne leaf 688x1004 (probed 2026-11-03; `/img/1600/`,
 * `/img/2048/` and `/img/full/` all return 404). A single-leaf band across the 1192px column is therefore a
 * **1.68x upscale that keeps 34% of the drawing** (`design/grill/round14/report.json`, `plates["band-mid"]`),
 * and record 0023 answered that in advance: *"if a strip is ever wanted from the archive, compose it from
 * several plates rather than upscale one."* Those three are each a whole leaf at **0.38–0.55x** of its native
 * scan. **That is no longer the shape on the page**, and under D351 it was no longer the width either - under D356 it is
 * again. With one plate the answer to "how wide may it go" was the scan under D347, became the **column** under D351,
 * and came back to the scan when the operator's letter of 2026-11-16 undid D351: the plate carries `max-width: 698px`,
 * so it runs to the column edge only where the column is narrower than the scan (every width under 1024) and stops at
 * its own scan above that. What survives of record 0023 is its other half, the one
 * that made this component's stylesheet necessary at all: a leaf whose own width is a percentage needs a **definite**
 * containing block, and left content-sized the shelf measured 434.35px inside a 1228px outer box at 1240 (the leaf sat
 * 0.62x under its own scan; @measured 2026-11-15 by `node design/plates-price.mjs`), so the shelf declares `width: 100%`
 * and the leaf reaches the edge instead of shrink-wrapping. What went is the exhibition, and what came is the cap's
 * removal - see the measured boxes above for what that costs at 1240.
 *
 * What it costs, measured on the built page at the box this component ships (`npm run round14`, option
 * `shelf-lg`, 2026-11-03): page **+321px at 1240** and **+321 at 1024**, **+0 at 640 and 390** (the band was
 * `hidden` below `lg` then), painted area **5.33% -> 7.47%** at 1240, and page chroma >= 0.08 **0.274% ->
 * 0.255%** — three more plates and the page gets *less* loud, because all three are colourless line work
 * (0% at chroma >= 0.08 each). @measured 2026-11-03 by npm run round14
 *
 * The alt text comes from the source's own picture page (`insideoutlibrary.com/p/<id>`, which carries book,
 * year and leaf), plus the one thing measured about the drawing itself: it is line work, ink at 21.1% of
 * pixels and 0% named colour (`design/grill/round14/report.json`). Nothing here describes a drawing nobody
 * has checked — an alt invented from a slot's mood is the D92 defect class in a `alt` attribute. The other two
 * leaves (`src/assets/photos/horizon-euclid1903.png`, `horizon-radio1922.png`) still sit in the asset folder and
 * still build from `scripts/plate-asset.mjs`: deleting artwork because no surface paints it is a different
 * change with its own sweep, and this task's fence is the surface — same ruling as the per-row event art (D343).
 */
const plate = {
  src: euler1910,
  // "euler1910" is our **slot key** from D149-2b (the line-work diagram that decision asked for), not a claim
  // about who drew it — the book is Slaught & Lennes 1910. The page's own text: "with center O tangent to the four
  // arcs as shown. Suggestion. Take X so that DX = XB. With centers A and B and radius AX draw arcs meeting at O."
  alt: 'Arcs cut from two centres, with a circle tangent to all four',
  provenance: 'Plane geometry, with problems and applications (Slaught & Lennes, 1910), leaf 122',
};
---

{/* `lg:px-6` rather than the usual `lg:px-8`: the measured band box is 384 CSS px per plate inside 24px side
    padding at 1240, and a different padding would make this markup a different page from the one the round
    measured. `data-slot="PS-5"` is a probe handle — check:space ranks slot boxes by it. */}
{/* `py-8` (32px), not `py-12`: the band's own air is part of what round 15 measured - its 128px of `py-16` was one
    of the two figures A3 was opened with (the other was a 283px hole inside the reasons section, and both are
    superseded by this ruling - the band now brings 64px after row F, and the reasons section's inside emptiness is
    186px, measured 2026-11-05 by `npm run check:space`, D220). Re-measured 2026-11-06 with two event rows on the page:
    reasons is still **186px**, and the largest run inside a surface on this page is the **join section's 274px** - a
    band moving cannot reach either of those, because both are inside a surface rather than between sections
    (D223). R6 took every landing-page band to a single 48px step (D214) on 2026-11-05; round 18's accepted row F
    (operator, 2026-11-17, S061) took this band and the other two below-fold bands down to 32px, so D214's one-step
    rule is superseded for these three bands while the hero and the surfaces inside them keep their own steps. The
    plates keep their box; only the air around the shelf moves. **Re-measured 2026-11-15 with ONE plate (D347), and
    the ladder did move: `check:space` on that build reads `/` at 390 as 10 runs >= 60px totalling 947px = 23.1% of
    a page it puts at 4100px, with 96px of the older `py-12` air inside the band's section box - the 2026-11-17
    ruling removes 32px of that air, so the 96px figure above is the pre-ruling number and the 4100px page is the
    pre-ruling page; this build's own figures are the ones `check:space` prints. The dated 186px and
    274px figures above are the two-event-row ladder of 2026-11-05/06 and are superseded by this run - five event
    rows and one plate change which surface holds the largest hole, so the only figure quoted here now is the one
    this build produces. The plate adds a surface between sections; it does not open a hole inside one.** */}
<section class="bg-paper py-8" aria-label="Plate from a public-domain mathematics book">
  {/* `flex` on the wrapper is not styling: check:space records a `data-slot` box only for a child of a grid or
      flex container, so a PS box inside a plain block is invisible to the survey and to every design round that
      reads it. Measured 2026-11-03: PS-5 was absent from the slot list until its parent became a flex container. */}
  <div class="max-w-screen-xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-6 flex">
    <div class="horizon" data-slot="PS-5">
      {/* A plate with a source is a figure (D312, 2026-11-14): the description is the `alt`, the source is the
          caption. D170's rule is that alt text is quoted from the picture page, never inferred; putting the book,
          year and leaf in the caption lets the alt say what a visitor sees instead of where the file came from. */}
      <figure class="horizon__plate">
        <Image src={plate.src} alt={plate.alt} class="horizon__img" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        <figcaption class="mt-2 text-micro leading-prose text-ink-3 font-body">{plate.provenance}</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* A shelf, not a grid, and since D347 the shelf holds ONE leaf: the plate keeps its own aspect (a leaf is
     698x350 at the archive's widest rung, aspect 1.99) and crops nothing, because cropping is how a diagram
     loses its labels. gap and align stay declared so a reader of the CSS sees the same box the round measured
     even though one child now fills it. */
  .horizon {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    width: 100%;
    gap: 1rem;
    align-items: flex-end;
    justify-content: center;
  }
  /* The plate width is written HERE rather than as utilities on the element, and it has to be: Astro compiles
     a component <style> selector to `.horizon__plate[data-astro-cid-…]`, specificity (0,2,0), which beats every
     Tailwind utility (0,1,0) - a `w-full` on the figure would lose to the `width` below it and quietly do nothing
     (measured 2026-11-15: the round-3 read asked for 3-up / 2-up / 1-up and the utilities changed nothing).
     The cap is BACK, and this is whose undo it is: the operator's rulings letter of 2026-11-16 (R2) rules that
     D351's cap deletion is undone and D347 is reinstated, because "full-bleed" names a visual outcome the archive
     cannot supply and this site's letter rule says the archive wins where it cannot supply the pixels. So the figure
     carries `max-width: 698px` - the leaf's own scan at the archive's widest rung - and never paints above 1.0x that
     scan. `width: 100%` stays: below 698px of column (every width under 1024) the plate still runs to the column
     edge, and it is the *ceiling* that changes, not the floor. What the cap costs is measured, not argued (see the
     header): the plate and its caption add +377px of page at 1240, band 472.8px against D351's 721.2px, and `check:ground`
     clear the legibility bars on the bytes a browser paints. The old cap's own
     record - the shelf measured 434.35px with no declared width, so the leaf sat 0.62x under its scan - stays in
     the header above, because it is why a percentage width needs a definite containing block at all. */
  .horizon__plate {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 698px;
  }
  .horizon__img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }
</style>
